@page "/learn/wasm-env-vars-config"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Server settings for WebAssembly · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Server settings for WebAssembly</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You build your WebAssembly app once. How does it find its API on each server?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> the server can now hand settings to a WebAssembly app in C#, with
        <code>BrowserOptions</code>. The app reads them with <code>IConfiguration</code>, like any other setting, and
        they beat the same key in <code>appsettings.json</code>. In .NET 10 you needed a hand-written start-up script,
        plus a call to <code>AddEnvironmentVariables()</code> and the <code>EnvironmentVariables</code> package in the
        client.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Recent orders</i> list that runs in the browser. It gets its API address with
        <code>Config["Orders:ApiUrl"]</code>. The build's <code>wwwroot/appsettings.json</code> points at a staging
        server that doesn't exist. The real server knows the right address. It sends it with the page as
        <code>Orders__ApiUrl</code>, a value that .NET in the browser treats as an environment variable.
    </p>
    <p>
        That value is <code>demo/env-config/api/orders</code>. It's a relative path, so the browser treats it as
        "this server's orders API".
    </p>
    <p>
        .NET 11 changed how WebAssembly apps read these values, so the .NET 10 version can't run live. Fig. 1 replays what a real .NET 10 app
        did when its server sent the same variable.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The list on .NET 10" Url="demo/env-config/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            This is a recording, not a live run. The <b>Variable</b> column shows that the server's
            value, <b>demo/env-config/api/orders</b>, arrived. But <b>Config[key]</b> still gave the staging URL, so the list
            failed to load. The grey note is the .NET 10 workaround, covered below.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Only the file was read</h2>
    <p>In .NET 10, a WebAssembly app's settings came from one place: the JSON file.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What a .NET 10 client's IConfiguration read</span>
        <pre class="snip">IConfiguration
├─ wwwroot/appsettings.json   <span class="dim">Orders:ApiUrl = https://staging-api…</span>     <span class="ok">✓ read</span>
└─ environment variables      <span class="dim">Orders__ApiUrl = demo/env-config/api/orders</span>  <span class="err">never read</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The variable did reach the app. <code>Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable</code> could see it. But
        <code>IConfiguration</code>, where the rest of the app looks for settings, only saw the file. And the server
        could only send the variable from a hand-written <code>Blazor.start</code> script. There was no C# way.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Set it in C#, read it as a setting</h2>
    <p>
        The server adds the variable to the settings it passes to the browser with the page. The client code stays
        the same.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// Server: the demo page</span>
<span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="ordersApi" /&gt;</span>

static readonly BrowserOptions ordersApi = new()
{
    InteractiveWebAssembly =
    {
        <span class="hl">EnvironmentVariables = { ["Orders__ApiUrl"] = OrdersApi.Url }</span>
    }
};

<span class="dim">// Client: plain configuration, no extra line or package</span>
apiUrl = Config["Orders:ApiUrl"];</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The server writes the variables into the page. When WebAssembly starts, it picks them up.
        <code>WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault</code> adds them to configuration after the
        <code>appsettings</code> files, so the variable overrides the file. Here's the same list on .NET 11.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same list on .NET 11" Url="demo/env-config/orders"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Wait for <b>Running on WebAssembly</b>. <b>Config[key]</b> now shows the server's value, and the orders
            load. <code>Orders:PageSize</code> has no variable, so it still comes from the file. Then click
            <b>Build's own settings</b>, which stops the server sending the variable. The app falls back to the
            staging URL, and the list fails to load, as it did on .NET 10.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two files behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/EnvConfig/RecentOrdersPage.razor" Feature="wasm-env-vars-config">
        <Caption>
            The server page. It sends <code>Orders__ApiUrl</code> with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>, unless
            the link has <code>?variable=off</code> (the second state in Fig. 2).
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/EnvConfig/RecentOrders.razor" Feature="wasm-env-vars-config">
        <Caption>
            The client component. <code>Config["Orders:ApiUrl"]</code> is the same line that gave staging in Fig. 1.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>A file per server, or a script and a package</h2>
    <p>In .NET 10, people tried three things to let the server choose. Only the last one worked:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">.NET 10 workarounds, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// wwwroot/appsettings.Production.json</span>
{ "Orders": { "ApiUrl": "https://…" } }</pre>
                <span class="why">every server's URL baked into the build</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a new server or a changed URL means building and deploying again</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"
        <span class="hl">autostart="false"</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
  Blazor.start({ webAssembly: {
    configureRuntime: dotnet =&gt;
      dotnet.<span class="hl">withEnvironmentVariable</span>(
        "Orders__ApiUrl", "@@apiUrl") } });
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the variable arrived, but <code>Config["Orders:ApiUrl"]</code> still returned the file's staging URL</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// Client Program.cs, plus the same script</span>
builder.Configuration
    .<span class="hl">AddEnvironmentVariables()</span>;
<span class="dim">// + package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration
//   .EnvironmentVariables (without it: CS1061)</span></pre>
                <span class="why">one line and one package in the client</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ returns the server's address</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        So it worked, but only with a script on the server, plus <code>AddEnvironmentVariables()</code> and its
        package in the client. There was no <code>BrowserOptions</code> to do it in C#.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A server setting, read like any other</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the server sets variables in C# with <code>BrowserOptions</code>, and the client reads them through
        <code>IConfiguration</code>. No script, no extra line, no package. That's Fig. 2.
    </p>
    <p>
        To set a variable for every page, not just one, use <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> on
        <code>MapRazorComponents</code> instead.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Watch out in RC2: use one or the other</span>
        <pre class="snip">app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()
   .<span class="hl">WithBrowserOptions</span>(...);       <span class="dim">// app-wide</span>
&lt;<span class="hl">ConfigureBrowser</span> Options="..." /&gt;   <span class="dim">// per page</span></pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ with both, one page's settings end up on every page, for every visitor</div>
        <p>
            This demo uses only the per-page one.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>A breaking change.</b> A variable now overrides the same key in <code>appsettings.json</code>. If your app already sent variables from <code>Blazor.start</code>, they'll now show up in <code>IConfiguration</code> too. Check for stray ones when you upgrade.</li>
        <li><b>Double underscore means colon.</b> <code>Orders__ApiUrl</code> becomes the key <code>Orders:ApiUrl</code>, same as on the server.</li>
        <li><b>Read once, at start.</b> The app reads its variables when WebAssembly starts. That's why the demo's links reload the whole page.</li>
        <li><b>Works with or without prerendering.</b> The variables are in the page either way. The demo turns off prerendering, where the server draws the page first (<code>prerender: false</code>, Fig. 3), so every value you see comes from the browser.</li>
        <li><b>Not for secrets.</b> The value is written into the page, so anyone can read it. Send addresses and on/off flags, never API keys or passwords.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
